IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cidra-types`

-- Consumer Insurance (Disclosure and Representations) Act 2012 (UK) — GOALS
-- 1 AND 2: DOES THE ACT APPLY; IS THERE A QUALIFYING MISREPRESENTATION, AND
-- OF WHICH CLASS (ss 1-5, 12(4)).
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-2012-6.txt.

§ `Goal 1 — does the Act apply`

-- s 1 "“consumer insurance contract” means a contract of insurance between—
-- an individual who enters into the contract wholly or mainly for purposes
-- unrelated to the individual's trade, business or profession, and a person
-- who carries on the business of insurance and who becomes a party to the
-- contract by way of that business". s 12(4) "This Act applies only in
-- relation to consumer insurance contracts entered into, and variations to
-- consumer insurance contracts agreed, after the Act comes into force."
@ref ss 1, 12(4)
@export Whether the Consumer Insurance (Disclosure and Representations) Act 2012 (UK) applies to a contract: a consumer insurance contract (an individual, wholly or mainly for purposes unrelated to their trade, business or profession, with an insurer acting by way of its insurance business) entered into after the Act came into force. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN k IS A `Contract facts`  @desc The parties, the purpose, and whether the contract came after commencement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies to` k IF
        k's `the policyholder is an individual`
    AND k's `wholly or mainly for purposes unrelated to their trade, business or profession`
    AND k's `the insurer is party by way of an insurance business`
    AND k's `entered into after the Act came into force`

§ `Goal 2 — the misrepresentation`

-- s 2(2) "It is the duty of the consumer to take reasonable care not to make
-- a misrepresentation to the insurer." s 3(5) "A misrepresentation made
-- dishonestly is always to be taken as showing lack of reasonable care."
@ref ss 2(2), 3(5)
GIVEN m IS A `Misrepresentation facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the duty in s 2(2) was breached by` m IF
        m's `a misrepresentation was made`
    AND (   NOT (m's `the consumer took reasonable care`)
         OR m's `made dishonestly`)

-- s 4(1) "An insurer has a remedy ... only if— (a) the consumer made the
-- misrepresentation in breach of the duty set out in section 2(2), and (b)
-- the insurer shows that without the misrepresentation, that insurer would
-- not have entered into the contract ... at all, or would have done so only
-- on different terms." A higher premium is a different term.
@ref s 4(1)-(2)
@export Whether a consumer's misrepresentation is a qualifying misrepresentation under s 4 of the Consumer Insurance (Disclosure and Representations) Act 2012 (UK): made in breach of the duty of reasonable care (s 2(2); dishonesty always shows lack of care, s 3(5)), and without it the insurer would not have entered into the contract at all or only on different terms (including a higher premium). DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Misrepresentation facts`             @desc Whether a misrepresentation was made, the finding on reasonable care, and dishonesty
      w IS A `What the insurer would have done`    @desc What the insurer would have done without it
      x IS A `Money facts`                         @desc The premium actually charged
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a qualifying misrepresentation` m `given` w `and` x IF
        `the duty in s 2(2) was breached by` m
    AND inducement
    WHERE
        inducement MEANS
            CONSIDER w
            WHEN `the same contract on the same terms` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `no contract on any terms` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `the contract, on these terms` different higher THEN
                different OR higher GREATER THAN x's `the premium actually charged`

-- s 5(2) "A qualifying misrepresentation is deliberate or reckless if the
-- consumer— (a) knew that it was untrue or misleading, or did not care
-- whether or not it was untrue or misleading, and (b) knew that the matter
-- to which the misrepresentation related was relevant to the insurer, or did
-- not care whether or not it was relevant to the insurer." (5) "it is to be
-- presumed, unless the contrary is shown— (a) that the consumer had the
-- knowledge of a reasonable consumer, and (b) that the consumer knew that a
-- matter about which the insurer asked a clear and specific question was
-- relevant to the insurer." Where neither is shown (NOTHING), the
-- presumptions answer.
@ref s 5(2), (5)
GIVEN m IS A `Misrepresentation facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the consumer knew it untrue, or did not care, under` m IF
    CONSIDER m's `knew it untrue, or did not care`
    WHEN JUST b  THEN b
    WHEN NOTHING THEN m's `a reasonable consumer would have known it untrue`

@ref s 5(2), (5)
GIVEN m IS A `Misrepresentation facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the consumer knew it relevant, or did not care, under` m IF
    CONSIDER m's `knew it relevant, or did not care`
    WHEN JUST b  THEN b
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
            m's `asked a clear and specific question about it`
        OR  m's `a reasonable consumer would have known it relevant`

DECLARE `Class of misrepresentation` IS ONE OF
    `deliberate or reckless`
    `careless`

-- s 5(1) "a qualifying misrepresentation ... is either— (a) deliberate or
-- reckless, or (b) careless." (3) "A qualifying misrepresentation is careless
-- if it is not deliberate or reckless."
@ref s 5(1)-(5)
@export The class of a qualifying misrepresentation under s 5 of the Consumer Insurance (Disclosure and Representations) Act 2012 (UK): deliberate or reckless if the consumer knew it was untrue or misleading (or did not care) and knew the matter was relevant to the insurer (or did not care), with the s 5(5) presumptions where neither is shown; otherwise careless. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Misrepresentation facts`  @desc What the consumer was shown to know, and the facts for the presumptions
GIVETH A `Class of misrepresentation`
`the class of` m MEANS
    IF       `the consumer knew it untrue, or did not care, under` m
         AND `the consumer knew it relevant, or did not care, under` m
    THEN `deliberate or reckless`
    ELSE `careless`
